  "body": "Requested alongside the standard vises (see the sibling issue). Common **5-axis self-centering vises**, where both jaws move to the centre, often with dovetail jaws, are a different shape: short, tall and narrow, to leave tool clearance for tilted work.\n\n**What:** generic parametric self-centering vises in the fixture library at the common jaw widths, **52, 75, 80 and 125-130 mm**:\n- jaw width and height;\n- clamping range;\n- body length, width and height.\n\nThe clamped width is set from the stock, centred on the vise. The geometry is original and simple: a body, two jaws, an optional dovetail lip.\n\n**Why separately:** they're used on tilting and rotary setups, so the collision checks have to see them in the posed/rotary frame (#339 slice 3) as well.\n\nSizes from a first search: 52 mm (clamping about 3.5-38.5 mm), 75 mm (about 0-40 mm), 80 mm (about 0-127 mm), and 125-130 mm (about 0-208 mm on long bodies). Confirm them from public spec sheets and record them as typical values.\n\nEffort: high.\n\n**Done means:**\n- The four sizes can be placed, centred on the stock.\n- Their mesh is judged in both upright and tilted setups.\n- Pinned by a tilted-tool collision test.\n\nPart of #339.\n",
